// A native enum reached through a **group** import (`use std.http` → `http.Framing.Sse`) is the
// same value as one reached through a leaf import (`use std.http.{Framing}` → `Framing.Sse`).
//
// The qualified spelling used to be unreachable: it type-checked and then died at *compile* time
// with `internal error: the VM cannot compile this program: type member used as a value` — a
// namespace handle was registered as an opaque *type*, so `http.Framing` lowered as a member access
// on it. That is the same "a variant that checks clean is not a value" hole as the prelude enums,
// on the extension-registered path. It matters because two packages can export the same short name
// (`std.http.Framing` and a provider package's `Framing`), and then the dotted spelling is the only
// one a program that needs both can write.
//
// Both spellings must agree on *identity*, not just on compiling: the loader rewrites the dotted
// prefix to the type's qualified identity, both backends register it under that key, and the value
// they build carries the type's short runtime name — the name a native-returned variant stamps. So
// the four combinations below (equality, `is`, `match` across spellings, `type_of`) all line up.
// expect: stdout "Framing.Sse"
// expect: stdout "Framing.Sse"
// expect: stdout "eq: true"
// expect: stdout "is: true"
// expect: stdout "match: sse"
// expect: stdout "type: Type.Enum(std.http.Framing, [])"
// expect: stdout "type: Type.Enum(std.http.Framing, [])"
// expect: exit 0
use std.http
use std.http.{Framing}

qualified = http.Framing.Sse;
leaf = Framing.Sse;

echo qualified;
echo leaf;
echo "eq: ${qualified == leaf}";
echo "is: ${qualified is Framing}";
echo "match: ${match qualified { Framing.Sse => "sse", _ => "other" }}";
echo "type: ${type_of(qualified)}";
echo "type: ${type_of(leaf)}";
